"lectures/kaplansky.tex at ec842e6a09851e98a38e2a4bea3b273b4a9debb9 · gilesgardam/lectures · GitHub (https://github.com/gilesgardam/lectures/blob/ec842e6a09851e98a38e2a4bea3b273b4a9debb9/kaplansky.tex)\nciteturn1360view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1358view2\",\"lineno\":2255}); Total lines: 3699\nL2201: Note first that for $k \\in K \\setminus \\{0\\}$ and $g \\in G$, the element $kg \\in K[G]$ is a unit (with $(k \\cdot g)^{-1} = k^{-1} \\cdot g^{-1}$); such units are called \\emph{trivial}.\nL2202: \nL2203: A group is called \\emph{torsion-free} if it has no non-trivial finite order elements.\nL2204: \nL2205: For example, fundamental groups of aspherical manifolds are torsion-free.\nL2206: \nL2207: \\begin{definition}\nL2208: \nL2209: Let $\\mathcal{P}$ be a property of groups.\nL2210: A group $G$ is \\emph{virtually $\\mathcal{P}$} if it has a finite-index subgroup $G_0$ with $\\mathcal{P}$.\nL2211: \nL2212: \\end{definition}\nL2213: \nL2214: As another example, a finitely generated subgroup of $\\GL_n(\\C)$ is virtually torsion-free (Selberg).\nL2215: \nL2216: \\begin{conjecture}[The Kaplansky conjectures]\nL2217: \nL2218: If $G$ is torsion-free, then $K[G]$ has\nL2219: \nL2220: \\begin{itemize}\nL2221: \nL2222: \\item no non-trivial units: $\\alpha \\beta = \\beta \\alpha = 1 \\implies \\alpha = kg$ for some $k \\in K^\\times, g \\in G$\nL2223: \\item no non-zero zero divisors: $\\alpha \\beta = 0 \\implies \\alpha = 0$ or $\\beta = 0$, and\nL2224: \nL2225: \\item no non-trivial idempotents: $\\alpha^2 = \\alpha \\implies \\alpha = 0$ or $\\alpha = 1$.\nL2226: \nL2227: \\end{itemize}\nL2228: \nL2229: For \\emph{any} $G$ (possibly with torsion), $K[G]$ is\nL2230: \nL2231: \\begin{itemize}\nL2232: \nL2233: \\item directly finite: $\\alpha \\beta = 1 \\implies \\beta \\alpha = 1$.\nL2234: \nL2235: \\end{itemize}\nL2236: \nL2237: \\end{conjecture}\nL2238: \nL2239: These are the unit conjecture, zero divisor conjecture, idempotent conjecture and direct finiteness conjectures respectively.\nL2240: (Direct finiteness is also called \\emph{Dedekind finiteness} or \\emph{von Neumann finiteness}.)\nL2241: \nL2242: \\begin{remark}\nL2243: \nL2244: The unit conjecture is false; the others are open.\nL2245: \nL2246: \\end{remark}\nL2247: \nL2248: \\begin{remark}\nL2249: \nL2250: Torsion-freeness is essential. For example, if $g \\in G$ has order $n \\geq 2$ then $(1 - g)(1 + g + \\dots + g^{n-1}) = 1 - g^n = 0$.\nL2251: \nL2252: \\end{remark}\nL2253: \nL2254: \\begin{remark}\nL2255: \nL2256: These conjectures are ``local'' in the sense that they only depend on the finitely generated subgroups of $G$.\nL2257: \nL2258: \\end{remark}\nL2259: \nL2260: \\begin{proposition}\nL2261: \\label{proposition:kaplansky_relations}\nL2262: \nL2263: For a given torsion-free group $G$ and field $K$, we have \\[\nL2264: \nL2265: \\text{unit conj.} \\implies\nL2266: \nL2267: \\text{zero divisor conj.} \\implies\nL2268: \nL2269: \\text{idempotent conj.} \\implies\nL2270: \nL2271: \\text{direct finiteness}\nL2272: \nL2273: \\]\nL2274: \nL2275: \\end{proposition}\nL2276: \nL2277: \\begin{proof}\nL2278: The 3 weaker conjectures are ring theoretic statements and their implications are easy ring theoretic observations: if $\\alpha \\beta = 1$ and $\\beta \\alpha \\neq 1$ then $(\\beta \\alpha)^2 = \\beta (\\alpha \\beta) \\alpha = \\beta \\alpha$ is a non-trivial idempotent.\nL2279: \nL2280: If $\\alpha^2 = \\alpha$ is a non-trivial idempotent, then $\\alpha^2 - \\alpha = \\alpha (\\alpha - 1) = 0$ with both factors non-zero.\nL2281: The unit conjecture is a ``group ring theoretic'' statement and the proof of the implication requires the following (which we'll prove later using group theory):\nL2282: \nL2283: \\begin{theorem}[Connell]\nL2284: \nL2285: $K[G]$ is prime if and only if $G$ has no non-trivial finite normal subgroup.\nL2286: \nL2287: \\end{theorem}\nL2288: \nL2289: A non-commutative ring $R$ is called \\emph{prime} if the zero ideal is not a product of two non-zero ideals.\nL2290: This is equivalent to saying that for all $0 \\neq \\alpha, \\beta \\in R$ there exists $\\gamma \\in R$ with $\\alpha \\gamma \\beta \\neq 0$.\nL2291: \nL2292: Since $G$ is torsion-free, $K[G]$ is prime.\nL2293: \nL2294: Suppose that $\\alpha \\beta = 0$ for $\\alpha, \\beta \\neq 0$.\nL2295: \nL2296: Then there exists $\\gamma \\in K[G]$ with $\\beta \\gamma \\alpha \\neq 0$.\nL2297: \nL2298: Now \\[\nL2299: \nL2300: (1 + \\beta \\gamma \\alpha) (1 - \\beta \\gamma \\alpha) = 1 - \\beta \\gamma (\\alpha \\beta) \\gamma \\alpha = 1.\nL2301: \nL2302: \\]\nL2303: Thus $1 + \\beta \\gamma \\alpha$ is a non-trivial unit, since if it were trivial then $\\beta \\gamma \\alpha = kg - 1$ which implies $k^2 g^2 - 2kg + 1 = 0$ which is absurd unless $g = 1$, which then forces $\\beta \\gamma \\alpha \\in K$ so $\\beta \\gamma \\alpha = 0$ since it is nilpotent.\nL2304: \nL2305: \\end{proof}\nL2306: \nL2307: \\begin{definition}\nL2308: \nL2309: A group $G$ is \\emph{residually finite} if for all $1 \\neq g \\in G$ there exists a homomorphism $\\phi_g \\colon G \\to Q$ to a finite group such that $\\phi_g (g) \\neq 1$.\nL2310: \nL2311: \\end{definition}\nL2312: We will see later that the direct finiteness conjecture is true for $K = \\C$.\nL2313: \nL2314: Here we prove:\nL2315: \nL2316: \\begin{proposition}\nL2317: \nL2318: \\label{proposition:rf_implies_df}\nL2319: \nL2320: Let $G$ be residually finite.\nL2321: \nL2322: Then $K[G]$ is directly finite.\nL2323: \nL2324: \\end{proposition}\nL2325: \nL2326: \\begin{notation}\nL2327: \nL2328: For $\\alpha \\in K[G]$ and $g \\in G$, let $(\\alpha)_g \\in K$ denote the coefficient of $g$ in $\\alpha$.\nL2329: \nL2330: Then $\\alpha = \\sum_{g \\in G} (\\alpha)_g \\cdot g$ and $(\\alpha)_g = 0$ for all but finitely many elements of $G$.\nL2331: \nL2332: \\end{notation}\nL2333: \nL2334: \\begin{definition}\nL2335: An element $\\alpha \\in K[G]$ has \\emph{support} \\[\nL2336: \nL2337: \\supp(\\alpha) = \\set{ g \\in G }{ (\\alpha)_g \\neq 0 }.\nL2338: \nL2339: \\]\nL2340: \nL2341: \\end{definition}\nL2342: \nL2343: \\begin{proof}\nL2344: \nL2345: Suppose $\\alpha, \\beta \\in K[G]$ with $\\alpha \\beta = 1$.\nL2346: \nL2347: A group homomorphism $\\phi \\colon G \\to Q$ induces a ring homomorphism $K[G] \\to K[Q]$.\nL2348: \nL2349: Thus $K[Q]$ is a $K[G]$-module.\nL2350: \nL2351: Note that $Q$ is a basis for the $K$-vector space $K[Q]$, so if $Q$ is finite this is a finite dimensional representation of $G$ on $V = K[Q]$.\nL2352: Let $A = \\supp(\\alpha)$, $B = \\supp(\\beta)$.\nL2353: \nL2354: Let $C = BA = \\set{ ba }{ a \\in A, b \\in B}$.\nL2355: \nL2356: By residual finiteness, there is a finite quotient $\\phi \\colon G \\epi Q$ which is injective on $C$: take the product of homomorphisms $\\phi_g$ given by the definition over all $g \\in C^{-1} C \\setminus \\{1\\}$ (and let $Q$ be the image of this product homomorphism).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/Kbook/Kbook.ii.pdf)\nciteturn1360view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 100; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1358view3\",\"lineno\":161}); Total lines: 6390\nL124@P2: If M is an abelian monoid, M−1M need not be partially ordered (by the image\nL125@P2: of M), because we may have [a] + [b] = 0 for a, b ∈ M. However, interesting\nL126@P2: examples are often partially ordered. For example, the Burnside ring A(G) and\nL127@P2: Representation ring R(G) are partially ordered (by G-sets and representations).\nL128@P2: When it exists, the ordering on M−1M is an extra piece of structure. For\nL129@P2: example, Z\nL130@P2: r\nL131@P2: is the group completion of both N\nL132@P2: r\nL133@P2: and M = {0} ∪ {(n1, ..., nr) ∈ N\nL134@P2: r\nL135@P2: :\nL136@P2: n1, ..., nr > 0}. However, the two partially ordered structures on Z\nL137@P2: r\nL138@P2-3: are different.4 II. THE GROTHENDIECK GROUP K0\nL139@P3: EXERCISES\nL140@P3: 1.1 Show that the group completion of a non-abelian monoid M is a group Mc,\nL141@P3: together with a monoid map M → Mc which is universal for maps from M to\nL142@P3: groups. Show that every monoid has a group completion in this sense, and that\nL143@P3: if M is abelian Mc = M−1M. If M is the free monoid on a set X, show that the\nL144@P3: group completion of M is the free group on the set X.\nL145@P3: 1.2 If M = M1×M2, show that M−1M is the product group (M−1\nL146@P3: 1 M1)×(M−12 M2).\nL147@P3: 1.3 If M is the filtered colimit of abelian monoids Mα, show that M−1M is the\nL148@P3: filtered colimit of the abelian groups M−1\nL149@P3: α Mα.\nL150@P3: 1.4 Mayer-Vietoris for group completions. Suppose that a sequence L → M1 ×\nL151@P3: M2 → N of abelian monoids is “exact” in the sense that whenever m1 ∈ M1 and\nL152@P3: m2 ∈ M2 agree in N then m1 and m2 are the image of a common ` ∈ L. If\nL153@P3: L is cofinal in both M1 and M2, show that there is an exact sequence L\nL154@P3: −1L →\nL155@P3: (M−1\nL156@P3: 1 M1) ⊕ (M−12 M2) → N −1N, where the first map is the diagonal inclusion and\nL157@P3: the second map is the difference map (m1, m2) 7→ m¯ 1 − m¯ 2.\nL158@P3: 1.5 Classify all abelian monoids which are quotients of N = {0, 1, . . . } and show\nL159@P3: that they are all finite. How many quotient monoids M = N/∼ of N have m\nL160@P3-4: elements and group completion Mc = Z/nZ?II. THE GROTHENDIECK GROUP K0 5\nL161@P4: §2. K0 of a ring\nL162@P4: Let R be a ring. The set P(R) of isomorphism classes of f.g. projective R\u0002modules, together with direct sum ⊕ and identity 0, forms an abelian monoid. The\nL163@P4: Grothendieck group of R, K0(R), is the group completion P−1P of P(R).\nL164@P4: When R is commutative, K0(R) is a commutative ring with 1 = [R], because\nL165@P4: the monoid P(R) is a commutative semiring with product ⊗R. This follows from\nL166@P4: the following facts: ⊗ distributes over ⊕; P ⊗R Q ∼= Q ⊗R P and P ⊗R R ∼= P; if\nL167@P4: P, Q are f.g. projective modules then so is P ⊗R Q (by Ex. I.2.7).\nL168@P4: For example, let F be a field or division ring. Then the abelian monoid P(F)\nL169@P4: is isomorphic to N = {0, 1, 2, . . . }, so K0(F) = Z. The same argument applies to\nL170@P4: show that K0(R) = Z for every local ring R by (I.2.2), and also for every PID (by\nL171@P4: the Structure Theorem for modules over a PID). In particular, K0(Z) = Z.\nL172@P4: The Eilenberg Swindle I.2.8 shows why we restrict to finitely generated projec\u0002tives. If we included R\nL173@P4: ∞, then the formula P ⊕R∞ ∼= R\nL174@P4: ∞ would imply that [P] = 0\nL175@P4: for every f.g. projective R-module, and therefore that K0(R) = 0.\nL176@P4: K0 is a functor from rings to abelian groups, and from commutative rings to\nL177@P4: commutative rings. To see this, suppose that R → S is a ring homomorphism. The\nL178@P4: functor ⊗RS: P(R) → P(S) (sending P to P ⊗R S) yields a monoid map P(R) →\nL179@P4: P(S), hence a group homomorphism K0(R) → K0(S). If R, S are commutative\nL180@P4: rings then ⊗RS: K0(R) → K0(S) is a ring homomorphism, because ⊗S: P(R) →\nL181@P4: P(S) is a semiring map:\nL182@P4: (P ⊗R Q) ⊗R S ∼= (P ⊗R S) ⊗S (Q ⊗R S).\nL183@P4: The free modules play a special role in understanding K0(R) because they are\nL184@P4: cofinal in P(R). By Corollary 1.3 every element of K0(R) can be written as [P] −\nL185@P4: [Rn] for some P and n. Moreover, [P] = [Q] in K0(R) iff P, Q are stably isomorphic:\nL186@P4: P ⊕ Rm ∼= Q ⊕ Rm for some m. In particular, [P] = [Rn] iff P is stably free. The\nL187@P4: monoid L of isomorphism classes of free modules is N iff R satisfies the Invariant\nL188@P4: Basis Property of Chapter 1, §1. This yields the following information about K0(R).\nL189@P4: Lemma 2.1. The monoid map N → P(R) sending n to Rn induces a group\nL190@P4: homomorphism Z → K0(R). We have:\nL191@P4: (1) Z → K0(R) is injective iff R satisfies the Invariant Basis Property (IBP);\nL192@P4: (2) Suppose that R satisfies the IBP (e.g., R is commutative). Then\nL193@P4: K0(R) ∼= Z iff every f.g. projective R-module is stably free.\nL194@P4: Example 2.1.1. Suppose that R is commutative, or more generally that there\nL195@P4: is a ring map R → F to a field F. In this case Z is a direct summand of K0(R),\nL196@P4: because the map K0(R) → K0(F) ∼= Z takes [R] to 1. A ring with K0(R) = Q is\nL197@P4: given in Exercise 2.12 below.\nL198@P4: Example 2.1.2 (Simple rings). Consider the matrix ring R = Mn(F) over a\nL199@P4: field F. We saw in Example I.1.1 that every R-module is projective (because it is\nL200@P4: a sum of copies of the projective module V ∼= F\nL201@P4: n), and that length is an invariant\nL202@P4: of finitely generated R-modules. Thus length is an abelian group isomorphism\nL203@P4: K0(Mn(F)) ∼=−→ Z sending [V ] to 1. Since R has length n, the subgroup of K0(R) ∼=\nL204@P4: Z generated by the free modules has index n. In particular, the inclusion Z ⊂ K0(R)\nL205@P4-5: of Lemma 2.1 does not split.6 II. THE GROTHENDIECK GROUP K0\nL206@P5: Example 2.1.3. (Karoubi) We say a ring R is flasque if there is an R-bimodule\nL207@P5: M, f.g. projective as a right module, and a bimodule isomorphism θ : R ⊕ M ∼= M.\nL208@P5: If R is flasque then K0(R) = 0. This is because for every P we have a natural\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputable permutations and word problems (https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/44364)\nciteturn1360view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/44364\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 876\nL0@P0: L’Enseignement Mathématique (2) 64 (2018), 143–160 DOI 10.4171/LEM/64-1/2-6\nL1@P0: Computable permutations and word problems\nL2@P0: Andrey Morozov and Paul Schupp\nL3@P0: Abstract. \u0018is is an expository paper whose goal is to explain some interesting intercon\u0002nections between group theory and the theory of computability. Let C denote the group\nL4@P0: of all computable permutations of N . A basic question is: What can one say about the\nL5@P0: \nL6@P0: nitely generated subgroups of C ? Partially answering this question involves ideas from the\nL7@P0: theory of computability such as Turing degrees and truth-table degrees. We want to make\nL8@P0: this paper accessible to both group theorists and computability theorists so we carefully\nL9@P0: explain all the needed concepts.\nL10@P0: Mathematics Subject Classi\nL11@P0: cation (2010). Primary: 20F36; Secondary: 03D35.\nL12@P0: Keywords. Computable permutation, Turing degree, truth-table degree, HNN extension.\nL13@P0: 1. Introduction\nL14@P0: We consider the group C of all computable permutations of N , so we have to\nL15@P0: begin with a precise de\nL16@P0: nition of “computable”. In his famous 1936 paper Turing\nL17@P0: [Tur1] formulated the idea of the universal digital computer, the Turing machine.\nL18@P0: It is not necessary to have in mind the exact details of how Turing machines\nL19@P0: work and one can think of such a machine as an ordinary computer with a\nL20@P0: xed\nL21@P0: program but with in\nL22@P0: nite memory and no time limit on computations. \u0018e crucial\nL23@P0: fact is that a Turing machine is completely de\nL24@P0: ned by its\nL25@P0: nite program and that\nL26@P0: there are therefore only countably many Turing machines and one can make an\nL27@P0: e\u001bective list of all of them. (For example, see [Soa] for precise details.) One\nL28@P0: does not need to have mind the exact working details of a Turing machine when\nL29@P0: thinking about computability and computability theorists don’t worry about them\nL30@P0: when proving theorems. It is now accepted that computable by a Turing machine\nL31@P0: is the correct precise de\nL32@P0: nition of the word “computable”.\nL33@P0: \u0018ere are essentially two ways to use Turing machines. First of course, to\nL34@P0: compute functions. If † and  are\nL35@P0: nite alphabets then †\u0003and \u0003 denote\nL36@P0-1: the set of all words over † and  respectively. A function f W †\u0003 ! \u0003is144 A. Morozov and P. Schupp\nL37@P1: computable if there exists a Turing machine M which, when started with an\nL38@P1: arbitrary input w 2 † halts with output f .w/. A set S \u0012 †\u0003is computable if\nL39@P1: its characteristic function is computable.\nL40@P1: \u0018e second basic way of using Turing machines is as enumerators. A set\nL41@P1: S \u0012 †\u0003is computably enumerable if there exists a Turing machine with a special\nL42@P1: output tape, which successively writes elements of †\u0003, say separated by spaces,\nL43@P1: on its output tape, and the set of words eventually listed is exactly S . In other\nL44@P1: words, one can e\u001bectively list the elements of S . Note that there is no constraint\nL45@P1: on the order in which elements are listed and that a given element may be listed\nL46@P1: many times, even in\nL47@P1: nitely often.\nL48@P1: A\nL49@P1: rst basic lemma of computability theory is that a set S \u0012 †\u0003is computable\nL50@P1: if and only if both S and its complement S are computably enumerable. If both\nL51@P1: S and its complement S are computably enumerable, we can start running both\nL52@P1: enumerations. Any given word w will occur in exactly one of the enumerations\nL53@P1: and when it does, we can correctly answer if w 2 S . If S is computable, then\nL54@P1: an enumeration of either S or S is obtained by enumerating †\u0003\nL55@P1: and listing\nL56@P1: according to whether or not a word is in S . (Note that this proof uses no technical\nL57@P1: details about how Turing machines work.)\nL58@P1: Returning to the group C , note that C is countable since there are only\nL59@P1: countably many Turing machines. \u0018e general question which we consider is:\nL60@P1: What can one say about the\nL61@P1: nitely generated subgroups of C ? \u0018e word problem,\nL62@P1: WP .G/, of a\nL63@P1: nitely generated group G D hXI Ri is the set of all words over the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnits, zero-divisors and idempotents in rings graded by torsion-free groups (https://arxiv.org/html/1904.04847v3)\nciteturn1360view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1359view0\",\"pattern\":\"Problem 1\"}); Total lines: 622\nL36: Furthermore, a ring which is graded by an arbitrary torsion-free group is shown to be indecomposable, and to have no non-trivial central zero-divisor and no non-homogeneous central unit. We also present generalizations of the classical group ring conjectures.\nL37: ###### Key words and phrases:\nL38: \nL39: group graded ring, torsion-free group, unique product group, unit conjecture, zero-divisor conjecture, idempotent conjecture\nL40: \nL41: ###### 2020 Mathematics Subject Classification\nL42: \nL43: 16W50, 16S34, 16S35, 20F99, 16U40, 16U60, 16U70, 16U99\nL44: ## 1. Introduction\nL45: With a few exceptions, notably [【14†19】], the first articles on group rings of infinite groups appeared in the early 1950s. A key person in that line of research was I. Kaplansky, known for his many deep contributions to ring theory and operator algebra.\nL46: In his famous talk, given at a conference that was held on June 6-8, 1956 at Shelter Island, Rhode Island, New York, he proposed twelve problems in the theory of rings [【15†21】, 【16†22】], one of which has become known as the zero-divisor problem (for group rings).\nL47: Although popularized by Kaplansky, the zero-divisor problem and its corresponding conjecture had in fact already been introduced by G. Higman in his 1940 thesis [【17†18】, p. 77] (see also [【18†45】, p. 112]). In [【17†18】], Higman also introduced the so-called unit problem and the corresponding unit conjecture. A third problem which is closely related to the previous two, is the idempotent problem. For clarity, we now recall the exact formulation of the three problems.\nL48: ###### Problem 1 (Higman/Kaplansky).\nL49: \nL50: Let $K$ be a field, let $G$ be a torsion-free group and denote by $K[G]$ the corresponding group ring.\nL51: \nL52:   1. (a)\nL53: \nL54: Is every unit in $K[G]$ necessarily trivial, i.e. a scalar multiple of a group element?\nL55: \nL56:   2. (b)\nL57: \nL58: Is $K[G]$ necessarily a domain?\nL59: \nL60:   3. (c)\nL61: \nL62: Is every idempotent in $K[G]$ necessarily trivial, i.e. either $0$ or $1$?\nL63: Many of the problems in Kaplansky’s original list [【15†21】] have been solved. The zero-divisor problem and the unit problem have been solved, in the affirmative, for several important classes of groups (see e.g. [【19†7】, 【20†10】, 【21†13】, 【22†14】, 【23†24】, 【24†25】]). Significant progress has been made on the idempotent problem using algebraic as well as analytical methods (see e.g. [【25†8】, 【26†15】] and [【27†20】, 【28†28】, 【29†29】, 【30†42】]).\nL64: For a thorough account of the development on the above problems during the 1970s, we refer the reader to D. S. Passman’s extensive monograph [【31†39】]. In recent years computational approaches have been proposed as means of attacking the zero-divisor problem (see [【32†12】, 【33†46】]). In 2021, there was a major breakthrough when G. Gardam [【34†16】] presented a counterexample to the unit conjecture in characteristic 2. Soon thereafter, A. G.\nL65: Murray [【35†30】] presented counterexamples in arbitrary prime characteristic. Nevertheless, for a general group $G$ all three problems remain open for group rings of characteristic zero.\nL66: We should point out that the problems exhibit a (potential) hierarchy. Indeed, an affirmative answer to the unit problem, for a fixed group $G$, implies an affirmative answer to the zero-divisor problem, which in turn implies an affirmative answer to the idempotent problem for the same group $G$ (see [【36†50】, Rem. 1.1]).\nL67: In the last two decades the idempotent problem has regained interest, mainly due to its connection with the Baum-Connes conjecture in operator algebra (see e.g [【36†50】]) via the so-called Kadison-Kaplansky conjecture for reduced group C^{∗}-algebras. The idempotent problem is also connected to the Farrell-Jones conjecture (see [【37†2】]). Moreover, W.\nL68: Lück [【38†26】] has shown that if $G$ is a torsion-free group and $K$ is a subfield of $\\mathbb{C}$ which satisfies the Atiyah conjecture [【38†26】, Conj. 10.3] with coefficients in $K$, then the zero-divisor problem has an affirmative answer for $K[G]$. Altogether, this shows that Problem 【39†1】, in particular in characteristic zero, remains highly relevant to modern mathematics.\nL69: In this article we will consider Problem 【39†1】 from a more general point of view, namely that of group graded rings. Let $G$ be a group with identity element $e$. Recall that a ring $R$ is said to be $G$-graded (or graded by $G$) if there is a collection $\\{R_{g}\\}_{g\\in G}$ of additive subgroups of $R$ such that $R=\\oplus_{g\\in G}R_{g}$ and $R_{g}R_{h}\\subseteq R_{gh}$, for all $g,h\\in G$.\nL70: Furthermore, a $G$-graded ring $R$ is called strongly $G$-graded (or strongly graded by $G$) if $R_{g}R_{h}=R_{gh}$, for all $g,h\\in G$.\nL71: Notice that the group ring $K[G]$ may be equipped with a canonical strong $G$-grading by putting $R:=K[G]$ with $R_{g}:=Kg$, for $g\\in G$. With this in mind, it is natural to ask whether it would make sense to extend Problem 【39†1】 to the more general context of strongly group graded rings. It turns out that it does. In fact, we propose the following even more general set of problems which will be the main focus of this article.\nL72: ###### Problem 2.\nL73: \nL74: Let $G$ be a torsion-free group and let $R$ be a unital $G$-graded ring equipped with a non-degenerate (see Definition 【40†2.1】) $G$-grading such that $R_{e}$ is a domain.\nL75: \nL76:   1. (a)\nL77: \nL78: Under the assumption that $\\Char(R_{e})=0$, is every unit in $R$ necessarily homogeneous w.r.t. the given $G$-grading?\nL79: \nL80:   2. (b)\nL81: \nL82: Is $R$ necessarily a domain?\n"
